1. What is a SQL query?
A SQL query is a command used to retrieve and manipulate data stored in a relational database management system (RDBMS). It is a set of instructions that are written in SQL (Structured Query Language) and are used to retrieve data from one or more tables in a database.
2. Examples of SQL queries:
Here are some examples of SQL queries:
* SELECT \* FROM employees WHERE salary > 50000;
This query selects all columns from the "employees" table where the salary is greater than 50000.
* UPDATE employees SET salary = salary + 5000 WHERE id = 101;
This query updates the salary of the employee with id = 101 by adding 5000 to it.
* DELETE FROM employees WHERE id = 101;
This query deletes the row from the "employees" table where the id is 101.
* INSERT INTO employees (id, name, salary) VALUES (101, 'John Smith', 55000);
This query inserts a new row into the "employees" table with id = 101, name = 'John Smith' and salary = 55000.
* SELECT COUNT(\*) FROM employees;
This query returns the number of rows in the "employees" table.
* SELECT MAX(id) FROM employees;
This query returns the maximum value of the "id" column in the "employees" table.
* SELECT MIN(id) FROM employees;
This query returns the minimum value of the "id" column in the "employees" table.
* SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees;
This query returns the average value of the "salary" column in the "employees" table.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees);
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary is greater than the average salary of all employees.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary < (SELECT MIN(salary) FROM employees);
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary is less than the minimum salary of all employees.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary BETWEEN 50000 AND 60000;
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary is between 50000 and 60000.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary > 50000 AND salary < 60000;
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary is greater than 50000 and less than 60000.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary = (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees);
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary is equal to the average salary of all employees.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary != (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees);
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary is not equal to the average salary of all employees.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary LIKE '%50000%';
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary contains the string '50000'.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary NOT LIKE '%50000%';
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary does not contain the string '50000'.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary BETWEEN 50000 AND 60000;
This query selects all rows from the "employees" table where the salary is between 50000 and 60000.
* SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE salary > 50000 AND salary < 60000;
